Below is the conflict logic written in TypeScript, so the database only loads slots and all overlap checks happen in code. I haven't edited `slots.service.ts` yet. Blocking dependencies are left out.

**About "day hours only":** it means slots don't span multiple days, so a single sort by start time and one pass over each resource's slots is enough. No interval tree or time buckets are needed. I still compare the full `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss` strings rather than reducing them to hours or minutes. The comparison is just as cheap, and it stays correct for the seeded overnight "Night Clean" slot (yesterday 23:00 to today 02:00). If only the clock time were compared, that slot's end (02:00) would be earlier than its start (23:00), and overlap checks against it would be wrong.

## Shared helpers (top of `slots.service.ts`)

```ts
type SlotRow = Omit<SlotDto, 'conflicts'>;

const toRow = (s: SlotRow): SlotRow => ({
  id: s.id,
  name: s.name,
  start: s.start,
  end: s.end,
  resourceId: s.resourceId,
});

const overlaps = (
  a: Pick<SlotRow, 'start' | 'end'>,
  b: Pick<SlotRow, 'start' | 'end'>,
) => a.start < b.end && a.end > b.start;

function annotateConflicts(slots: SlotRow[]): SlotDto[] {
  const byId = new Map<number, SlotDto>(
    slots.map((s) => [s.id, { ...toRow(s), conflicts: [] }]),
  );
  const sorted = [...slots].sort((a, b) => a.start.localeCompare(b.start));

  let active: SlotRow[] = [];
  for (const slot of sorted) {
    active = active.filter((a) => a.end > slot.start);
    for (const other of active) {
      byId.get(slot.id)!.conflicts!.push(toRow(other));
      byId.get(other.id)!.conflicts!.push(toRow(slot));
    }
    active.push(slot);
  }

  return slots.map((s) => byId.get(s.id)!);
}
```

How `annotateConflicts` works:
- Slots are processed in start-time order.
- `active` holds the slots that haven't ended yet.
- For each new slot, slots ending at or before its start are dropped. This is also why back-to-back slots (one ends at 10:00, the next starts at 10:00) don't conflict.
- Every slot left in `active` overlaps the new one, so each pair is recorded on both slots.
- Cost is one sort plus one pass: O(n log n) plus the number of conflicts.

## `getSlots`: one load, then group and annotate in memory

```ts
async getSlots(): Promise<ResourceSlotsDto[]> {
  const slots = await this.manager.find(Slot, { order: { id: 'ASC' } });

  const byResource = new Map<number, SlotRow[]>();
  for (const s of slots) {
    const group = byResource.get(s.resourceId) ?? [];
    group.push(toRow(s));
    byResource.set(s.resourceId, group);
  }

  return [...byResource].map(([resourceId, group]) => ({
    resourceId,
    slots: annotateConflicts(group),
  }));
}
```

Filtering to today's slots, which `getSlots` will eventually need, is separate from this logic and can be added to the `find` call later.

## Writes: check only that resource's slots, inside a transaction

```ts
private async findConflicts(
  manager: EntityManager,
  candidate: Pick<SlotRow, 'start' | 'end' | 'resourceId'>,
  excludeId?: number,
): Promise<SlotDto[]> {
  const sameResource = await manager.find(Slot, {
    where: { resourceId: candidate.resourceId },
  });
  return sameResource
    .filter((s) => s.id !== excludeId && overlaps(candidate, s))
    .map((s) => ({ ...toRow(s), conflicts: [] }));
}

async addSlot(createSlot: CreateSlotDto): Promise<SlotDto> {
  return this.manager.transaction(async (tx) => {
    const conflicts = await this.findConflicts(tx, createSlot);
    if (conflicts.length) {
      throw new HttpException(conflicts, HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }
    const saved = await tx.save(Slot.create({ ...createSlot }));
    return { ...toRow(saved), conflicts: [] };
  });
}

async updateSlot(slotId: number, updateSlot: UpdateSlotTimesDto): Promise<SlotDto> {
  return this.manager.transaction(async (tx) => {
    const slot = await tx.findOneOrFail(Slot, { where: { id: slotId } });
    const candidate = { ...updateSlot, resourceId: slot.resourceId };

    const conflicts = await this.findConflicts(tx, candidate, slotId);
    if (conflicts.length) {
      throw new HttpException(conflicts, HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }

    slot.start = updateSlot.start;
    slot.end = updateSlot.end;
    await tx.save(slot);
    return { ...toRow(slot), conflicts: [] };
  });
}
```

Points about the write path:
- **Overlap rule:** reads and writes use the same `overlaps` function, so the two paths can't disagree about what counts as a conflict.
- **Response body:** `HttpException(conflicts, 409)` sends the plain array of conflicting slots. That is the format the requirements specify, and `client/src/api.ts` already accepts it (`body.conflicts ?? body`).
- **Transaction:** the check and the save run together, so two requests can't both pass the check and both save overlapping slots.
- **Updates:** the slot being moved is excluded with `excludeId`, so it never conflicts with itself.
- **Scale:** loading every slot for one resource is fine at this data size. If a resource grows to many days of bookings, the `find` could be narrowed to the candidate's date. The overlap check itself stays in code either way.

