What keeps your idea working is storing the slots themselves in each hour's bucket instead of a number. The bucket's length is your count. A bucket of length 1 is skipped, so slots alone in their hours are never compared. In buckets with more than one slot, the exact rule `start < other.end && end > other.start` removes the false positives.

```ts
const SECONDS_PER_HOUR = 60 * 60;

function annotateConflicts(slots: SlotDto[], day: string): SlotDto[] {
  const timed = slots
    .map((slot) => ({
      slot,
      start: secondOfDay(slot.start, day),
      end: secondOfDay(slot.end, day),
    }))
    .filter((t) => t.start < t.end);

  const hourRange = (start: number, end: number) => [
    Math.floor(start / SECONDS_PER_HOUR),
    Math.floor((end - 1) / SECONDS_PER_HOUR),
  ];

  const hours: (typeof timed)[] = Array.from({ length: 24 }, () => []);
  for (const t of timed) {
    const [first, last] = hourRange(t.start, t.end);
    for (let h = first; h <= last; h++) hours[h].push(t);
  }

  return timed.map(({ slot, start, end }) => {
    const conflicts = new Map<number, SlotDto>();
    const [first, last] = hourRange(start, end);
    for (let h = first; h <= last; h++) {
      if (hours[h].length < 2) continue;
      for (const other of hours[h]) {
        if (other.slot.id !== slot.id && start < other.end && end > other.start) {
          conflicts.set(other.slot.id, { ...other.slot, conflicts: [] });
        }
      }
    }
    return { ...slot, conflicts: [...conflicts.values()] };
  });
}
```

How it works:
- **Dropping other days:** the `filter` on `start < end` removes slots that don't touch `day`. That gives the "today only" behaviour, and the overnight slot still counts as running from 00:00 to 02:00.
- **Back-to-back slots:** a slot fills the hours from its start up to `end - 1`, so one ending at exactly 10:00 is not added to the 10:00 bucket.
- **Duplicates:** the `Map` keyed by id reports a slot once even when it shares several hours with another.

