One pitfall: comparing each slot only with the one right before it misses conflicts. Take 09:00–12:00, then 10:00–10:30, then 11:00–11:30. The last two don't overlap, but the first overlaps both. So during the pass, keep a list of the slots that haven't ended yet (the "active" ones):

```ts
function annotateConflicts(slots: SlotDto[], today: string): SlotDto[] {
  const dayStart = `${today}T00:00:00`;
  const todays = slots
    .filter((s) => s.start.slice(0, 10) <= today && s.end > dayStart)
    .map((s) => ({ ...s, conflicts: [] as SlotDto[] }))
    .sort((a, b) => a.start.localeCompare(b.start));

  let active: SlotDto[] = [];
  for (const slot of todays) {
    active = active.filter((other) => other.end > slot.start);
    for (const other of active) {
      slot.conflicts!.push({ ...other, conflicts: [] });
      other.conflicts!.push({ ...slot, conflicts: [] });
    }
    active.push(slot);
  }
  return todays;
}
```

How it works:
- **Sorting:** the start times are `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss` strings, so `localeCompare` sorts them by time. No parsing is needed, and the overnight slot from yesterday sorts first.
- **Today only:** the `filter` keeps slots that start today or earlier and end after today's midnight. That includes the overnight slot.
- **Removing finished slots:** before each slot, active slots ending at or before its start are dropped. That's also why back-to-back slots (one ends at 10:00, the next starts at 10:00) don't conflict.
- **Recording conflicts:** every slot still active started earlier and hasn't ended, so it overlaps the current slot. The pair is recorded on both slots.
- **Cost:** one sort plus one pass, O(n log n) plus the number of conflicts. The active list only grows when slots really overlap, so the work beyond the sort is proportional to the conflicts you have to return anyway.

